WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.680
Are young neuron's neurites truly equal?

2
00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:09.160
Or is one of them fixed as the axon from the start?

3
00:00:10.183 --> 00:00:14.543
Gary Banker's laboratory grew neurons from the embryonic rat hippocampus.

4
00:00:15.683 --> 00:00:17.943
Most are pyramidal cells of one broad type.

5
00:00:18.883 --> 00:00:22.403
Grown at low density, each neuron can be watched on its own.

6
00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:28.687
In 1988, Carlos Dotti, C. A. Sullivan and Banker described five stages.

7
00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:35.367
Stage one. Within hours of plating, the round cell spreads thin, flat veils of

8
00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:36.367
membrane.

9
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:41.647
Stage two. The veils condense into several short neurites, all alike.

10
00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:47.687
Stage three. After a day or two, one neurite grows much faster than the rest.

11
00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:49.707
It becomes the axon.

12
00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:56.290
Stage four. Over the next few days, the other neurites grow, taper and branch as

13
00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:57.290
dendrites.

14
00:00:57.933 --> 00:01:03.853
Stage five. In the second week and later, spines form, and synapses multiply and

15
00:01:03.853 --> 00:01:04.853
mature.

16
00:01:05.717 --> 00:01:07.077
Back to stage two.

17
00:01:07.937 --> 00:01:08.937
The neurites look alike.

18
00:01:09.777 --> 00:01:10.797
That could mean two things.

19
00:01:11.817 --> 00:01:14.217
Perhaps one was fixed for good as the axon.

20
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.810
Or perhaps the choice is open, and they are competing.

21
00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:22.133
A young neuron's cut tests whether the choice can be undone.

22
00:01:23.217 --> 00:01:25.117
Take a neuron just at stage three.

23
00:01:25.897 --> 00:01:29.297
Cut its leader back to the length of its siblings, erasing its lead.

24
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:34.620
If the axon was fixed for good, which process should grow out as the axon?

25
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:36.600
And if the neurites compete?

26
00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:47.493
If the choice were fixed, the stump should regrow as the axon, whatever its length.

27
00:01:48.453 --> 00:01:49.453
Often, it did not.

28
00:01:50.517 --> 00:01:56.777
In 1987, Carlos Dotti and Gary Banker found that another neurite, a future dendrite,

29
00:01:57.017 --> 00:01:58.457
could grow out as the axon.

30
00:01:59.417 --> 00:02:03.217
Then Kimberly Goslin and Banker cut axons to different lengths.

31
00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:08.730
When one remaining process was more than about 10 micrometres longer than all the

32
00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:10.590
others, it became the axon.

33
00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:16.330
When the remaining processes were nearly equal, the new axon could not be predicted.

34
00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:19.170
It could be any of them, the cut one included.

35
00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:21.930
So the choice can be undone.

36
00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:25.570
It behaves like a race, and length is part of the score.

37
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:30.380
Goslin and Banker proposed that uncut neurons choose the same way.

38
00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:36.030
The 10 micrometre figure belongs to these experiments in cultured neurons.

39
00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:38.650
It is not a rule for every neuron.

40
00:02:39.683 --> 00:02:41.883
The logic does not depend on the number.

41
00:02:42.823 --> 00:02:44.383
Get far enough ahead, and you win.

42
00:02:45.383 --> 00:02:46.863
Stage two is a race.

43
00:02:46.863 --> 00:02:50.503
Next question: what kind of race has only one winner?
